output:
To show Docker containers using more than 500MB of memory, you'll typically use `docker stats` and then process its output to filter based on memory usage.

`docker stats` provides real-time statistics, but we need a snapshot for filtering. We can achieve this with `--no-stream` and then format the output to make it easier to parse with tools like `awk`.

Here's how you can do it:

```bash
docker stats --no-stream --format "table {{.Name}}\t{{.MemUsage}}" | awk '
BEGIN {
    print "CONTAINER NAME\tMEMORY USAGE";
    print "----------------------------";
}
NR > 1 { # Skip the header line from docker stats
    name = $1;
    mem_str = $2; # e.g., "1.23GiB", "550.0MiB", "400.0KiB"

    # Extract the numeric value and unit
    # This regex is more robust for different unit lengths (B, KiB, MiB, GiB)
    if (match(mem_str, /^([0-9.]+)([KMGT]?iB|B)$/)) {
        value = substr(mem_str, RSTART, RLENGTH - length(gensub(/[^A-Za-z]/,"","g",mem_str)));
        unit = substr(mem_str, RSTART + length(value), RLENGTH);

        # Convert all to MiB for easy comparison
        mem_mib = 0;
        if (unit == "KiB") {
            mem_mib = value / 1024;
        } else if (unit == "MiB") {
            mem_mib = value;
        } else if (unit == "GiB") {
            mem_mib = value * 1024;
        } else if (unit == "B") { # Bytes
            mem_mib = value / (1024 * 1024);
        }

        # Check if memory usage is greater than 500MiB
        if (mem_mib > 500) {
            printf "%s\t\t%.2f MiB\n", name, mem_mib;
        }
    }
}'
```

**Explanation:**

1.  **`docker stats --no-stream --format "table {{.Name}}\t{{.MemUsage}}"`**:
    *   `docker stats`: The command to get resource usage statistics.
    *   `--no-stream`: Ensures that `docker stats` takes a single snapshot and exits, rather than continuously streaming output.
    *   `--format "table {{.Name}}\t{{.MemUsage}}"`: Formats the output.
        *   `table`: Creates a header line.
        *   `{{.Name}}`: The name of the container.
        *   `{{.MemUsage}}`: The memory usage string (e.g., `550.0MiB / 2GiB`). We will only use the first part of this.

2.  **`| awk '...'`**: Pipes the output of `docker stats` to `awk` for processing.

    *   **`BEGIN { ... }`**: This block runs once before processing any input. It prints a custom header for our filtered output.
    *   **`NR > 1 { ... }`**: This block executes for every line after the first one (skipping the header generated by `docker stats --format table`).
    *   **`name = $1; mem_str = $2;`**: `awk` automatically splits lines into fields. `$1` is the container name, and `$2` is the memory usage string (e.g., "1.23GiB").
    *   **`if (match(mem_str, /^([0-9.]+)([KMGT]?iB|B)$/)) { ... }`**: This is the core parsing logic.
        *   It uses a regular expression to extract the numeric `value` (e.g., "1.23") and the `unit` (e.g., "GiB") from `mem_str`. This regex handles `B`, `KiB`, `MiB`, `GiB`, `TiB`.
        *   `RSTART` and `RLENGTH` are special `awk` variables set by `match()`.
    *   **`if (unit == "KiB") { ... } else if (unit == "MiB") { ... } else if (unit == "GiB") { ... }`**: Converts the extracted memory value into a common unit (MiB - Megabytes) for comparison.
    *   **`if (mem_mib > 500) { ... }`**: Checks if the memory usage in MiB is greater than 500 MiB.
    *   **`printf "%s\t\t%.2f MiB\n", name, mem_mib;`**: If the condition is met, it prints the container name and its memory usage in MiB, formatted to two decimal places.

This script will give you a clear list of containers exceeding the 500MB memory threshold.
